Below is a **minimal, production-ready pattern** that lets you move the map to any coordinate (or bounds) with a smooth, animated “fly” when the user clicks a button.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – flyTo on button click</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
    .control {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px; left: 10px;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
      padding: 6px 10px;
      border-radius: 4px;
      font-family: sans-serif;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>
<div class="control" id="flyBtn">Fly to San Francisco</div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (light vector basemap)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any valid style URL
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // start somewhere in the Bay Area
    zoom: 10,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0,
    // optional: improve accessibility by forcing a minimum contrast for labels
    // (see the maplibre-cartography skill for halo settings)
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Define the target view
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const target = {
    // longitude, latitude (Web Mercator)
    center: [-122.4194, 37.7749],   // San Francisco downtown
    zoom: 14,                      // a bit closer
    bearing: 30,                   // rotate a little for visual interest
    pitch: 45,                     // tilt to give depth
    // duration is in ms; 1000 ms = 1 second
    duration: 1500,
    // easing function – MapLibre ships with a few built-ins.
    // "easeOut" feels natural for a fly-to.
    easing: t => t * (2 - t)      // same as d3.easeCubicOut
  };

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Hook the button → map.flyTo()
  // -------------------------------------------------
  document.getElementById('flyBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
    // The fly animation respects the current map state,
    // so you can call it repeatedly without resetting anything.
    map.flyTo(target);
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  (Optional) Keep the button keyboard-accessible
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // Add ARIA role & make it focusable – good for WCAG compliance.
  const btn = document.getElementById('flyBtn');
  btn.setAttribute('role', 'button');
  btn.setAttribute('tabindex', '0');
  btn.addEventListener('keydown', e => {
    if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') {
      e.preventDefault();
      map.flyTo(target);
    }
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Why `flyTo`?

| Feature | `flyTo` (vs. `jumpTo` / `easeTo`) |
|---------|-----------------------------------|
| **Smoothness** | Implements the classic “flight” algorithm (cubic-bezier-like easing, zoom-speed coupling) that feels natural on both desktop and mobile. |
| **Control** | You can set `center`, `zoom`, `bearing`, `pitch`, `duration`, and a custom `easing` function. |
| **Interruptible** | If the user starts panning/zooming while the animation is running, the flight stops cleanly and the map follows the new interaction. |
| **Accessibility** | Because the animation is time-based (duration in ms) you can keep it short enough (< 2 s) to satisfy WCAG 2.1 “animation-avoidance” guidance for users who are motion-sensitive. |

### Common Gotchas & How to Avoid Them

| Issue | Fix |
|-------|-----|
| **Labels disappear on imagery** | If you later switch the basemap to satellite imagery, make sure your label style uses a **dark semi-transparent halo** (`"text-halo-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.75)"`) and white text (`"text-color": "#fff"`). See the *Basemap Type Determines Label Colors* table in the `maplibre-cartography` skill. |
| **Data layers covering labels** | Insert any custom data layers **before the first symbol layer** (`map.addLayer(myLayer, firstSymbolId)`). This keeps your geometry under the basemap’s labels. |
| **Icon halos not showing** | Halos only work on **SDF sprites** (`addImage(name, img, {sdf: true})`). If you need a halo around a raster icon, bake the halo into the PNG/SVG itself. |
| **Too-fast animation for low-vision users** | Keep `duration` ≥ 1200 ms for large jumps, or provide a UI toggle that disables animation (`map.jumpTo(...)`). |
| **Map jumps when the button is pressed repeatedly** | Debounce the click or check `map.isMoving()` before calling `flyTo`. Example: `if (!map.isMoving()) map.flyTo(target);` |

### Extending the Pattern

* **Fly to a bounding box** – use `map.fitBounds([[west, south], [east, north]], {padding: 40, duration: 1200});`
* **Fly to a feature from a GeoJSON source** – retrieve the feature’s centroid (`turf.centroid`) and feed it to `flyTo`.
* **Dynamic target** – read the coordinates from a data-attribute on the button (`data-lng`, `data-lat`) so a single handler can serve many “fly” buttons.

```js
btn.addEventListener('click', e => {
  const lng = +e.target.dataset.lng;
  const lat = +e.target.dataset.lat;
  map.flyTo({center: [lng, lat], zoom: 13, duration: 1300});
});
```

That’s all you need to give users a smooth, accessible “fly-to” experience in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping!
